Looking at this problem, I need to prove that `K.Clamp(x,l,h) <= K.Clamp(y,l,h)` given `x <= y && l <= h`.

The `Clamp` function clamps a value between `l` and `h`. When `x <= y`, their clamped values maintain that ordering.

I have access to `A.Below`, `A.Within`, and `A.Above` lemmas from the Api module, which establish:
- `Below(x,l,h)`: when `x < l`, `Clamp(x,l,h) == l`
- `Within(x,l,h)`: when `l <= x <= h`, `Clamp(x,l,h) == x`
- `Above(x,l,h)`: when `h < x`, `Clamp(x,l,h) == h`

I need to case split on where `x` and `y` fall relative to `l` and `h`.

Cases for x: `x < l`, `l <= x <= h`, `h < x`
Cases for y: `y < l`, `l <= y <= h`, `h < y`

Let me structure the proof:

```dafny
if x < l {
  A.Below(x, l, h);  // Clamp(x,l,h) == l
  if y < l {
    A.Below(y, l, h);  // Clamp(y,l,h) == l, so l <= l ✓
  } else if y <= h {
    A.Within(y, l, h);  // Clamp(y,l,h) == y, so l <= y ✓
  } else {
    A.Above(y, l, h);  // Clamp(y,l,h) == h, so l <= h ✓
  }
} else if x <= h {
  A.Within(x, l, h);  // Clamp(x,l,h) == x
  if y <= h {
    A.Within(y, l, h);  // Clamp(y,l,h) == y, so x <= y ✓
  } else {
    A.Above(y, l, h);  // Clamp(y,l,h) == h, so x <= h ✓
  }
} else {
  A.Above(x, l, h);  // Clamp(x,l,h) == h
  A.Above(y, l, h);  // Clamp(y,l,h) == h, so h <= h ✓
}
```

